   465                                  ;
   466                                  ; A verify has no buffer of its own, and the pointer handed with one is
   467                                  ; not required to point anywhere.  DOS passes ES:BX = 0000:0000, because
   468                                  ; on a PC function 04h confirms the sectors read back and never moves a
   469                                  ; byte into memory -- there is nothing for a buffer to mean.
   470                                  ;
   471                                  ; Reading a sector there writes the disk straight over the interrupt
   472                                  ; vector table.  FORMAT's verify pass asks for eighteen of them at once,
   473                                  ; so the whole table goes, every vector with it, and the machine is dead
   474                                  ; where it stands -- no console, no tick, nothing left to report with.
   475                                  ;
   476                                  ; Read into the BIOS scratch buffer instead and throw the data away, the
   477                                  ; way the fixed disk path has always done it.  Overwriting the frame here
   478                                  ; is safe because the caller's registers are put back on the way out.
   479                                  ;
